From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 17:34:16 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 170/175] feat: capture-side film grain, stage G1 - the digital
 sensor model (CMOS / CCD / Phone)

G0 gave every family the same white Gaussian on a film-space cell grid, which is grain: a
size on the negative and a response curve over tone. A sensor is not that, and the first
thing the user saw when switching family was that it looked identical to film with the
colour turned up - per-pixel colour confetti, which no camera has ever produced. This
stage replaces the generator for the three digital families with the sensor itself.

The model (FilmGrainSpec.md section 3.4)

Per photosite and per channel: e = x * fullWell * (isoBase / iso) electrons, shot noise as
a Poisson draw below 20 electrons and a Gaussian of variance e above it, read noise from a
per-ISO figure, a static multiplicative PRNU and additive DSNU hashed from the SENSOR pixel
index with no seed in it - so the fixed pattern is the same in every frame of every take,
which is what makes it fixed-pattern noise and not grain - and an optional per-row banding
term. Nothing in it consults a strength curve: the ISO law is EMERGENT. Raising the ISO
shrinks the electron count, so shot noise grows relative to the signal and the read floor
stops being negligible, and shadows come out loudest and highlights cleanest by arithmetic
rather than by a hand-drawn ramp. That is why the digital families take Grain Strength as a
plain multiplier around 1 and do NOT get the film ISO curve on top: multiplying an emergent
law by a modelled one would count the ISO twice.

The normalisation. A perturbation of s stops changes a value by a relative ln2 * s, so
setting ln2 * sigmaStops * x = sigma_y gives sigmaStops = sigma_y / (x * ln2) - the spec's
sigma_ref = x * ln2 written the other way round. The generator therefore hands the shared
blend a unit-variance field and a per-channel amplitude in stops, and that amplitude IS the
response curve of section 3, computed instead of authored. The blend, the encoded-domain
bias term, the clip guard and the untouched-pixel path are exactly G0's, shared by both
families; only the generator differs.

Between the photosite and the pixel

- Integration. The virtual sensor is wider than the frame - a full-frame body at 6 um is
  6000 photosites against a 1920-pixel output - so nPix photosites fall under one output
  pixel and the variance divides analytically, no loop and no supersampling. The exponent
  is 0.7 rather than 1: demosaicing correlates neighbouring photosites first, so a real
  downscaled frame gains measurably less than the textbook 1/nPix. Documented at the
  #define, one number to change to get the textbook result back.
- White balance. A Bayer sensor is most sensitive in green, so developing multiplies red
  and blue up - about 1.9 and 1.6 in daylight - and multiplies their noise with them, which
  is where the colour in digital noise comes from. The camera's own colour temperature
  tilts those gains through the same Kelvin curve the white-balance effect uses, so setting
  tungsten in daylight makes the noise bluer, as it does on a real body. Green is the
  reference and is always 1, so only two numbers travel on the bus.
- Chroma noise reduction. This is the fix for the confetti. Every real pipeline denoises
  the colour part of the noise far harder than the brightness part, because the eye's
  chroma acuity is low and the artefact is cheap to remove; what survives is fine, almost
  monochrome grit with a slow, faint colour blotch under it. The luma component is kept per
  pixel, the chroma component is redrawn on a coarser cell (up to 4 output pixels, from the
  preset) and attenuated. Exposed as one property, Chroma NR, so the raw confetti can be
  seen once at 0 and understood.

The two variants are parameters plus one shader term each. CCD: no dual gain, a read floor
of 12 electrons, heavier fixed pattern, and a column-correlated term gated by brightness -
one draw per column, static within the frame - which reads as the vertical streaking a CCD
shows. True smear needs the brightest value in the column and therefore a pass of its own;
this is the honest cheap stand-in and the comment says so. Phone: a 1 um photosite that
collects almost nothing, rescued computationally by stacking six frames, which is the same
variance division one level up. ADC quantisation is not modelled - the 8-bit write at the
end of the pass already quantises harder than an 8-to-12-bit ADC would.

Family selection travels in vGrainDebug.z, not in a new static flag. There was exactly one
free %_RT_ bit on this technique and the block itself spent it; a second flag would double
the composition pass' permutation count for a branch on a value that is uniform over the
whole frame.

r_FilmGrainDebug 2

The response, made visible instead of plotted. The bottom sixth of the frame becomes a grey
ramp running twelve stops from black to display white with the whole block running on it,
so the grain is shown at every tone at once, and the effective per-channel response is
drawn over it as a line in that channel's own colour on a ten-stop logarithmic axis. Film
shows the authored bump peaking in the mid-tones; a digital family shows its emergent
curve falling from left to right. The picture above the strip is untouched. The UPLOADED
response LUT of section 4 is still not implemented: it needs the plugin texture-id plumbing
that arrives with the plates in G2, and the built-in curve stays until then.

Presets and the bus

A C++ table keyed by family in ApplyFilmGrain, numbers only, no assets - CMOS (6 um,
60 000 e-, base ISO 800, 3 e- read dropping to 1.5 e- at the ISO 3200 dual-conversion-gain
step), CCD (8.4 um, 40 000 e-, base ISO 200, 12 e- read, no dual gain, column streak) and
Phone (1 um, 6 000 e-, base ISO 50, 1.2 e- read, six frames stacked). The photosite COUNT
follows the body: the pitch is the sensor's property, so a full frame at 6 um is 6000 sites
and an APS-C body of the same technology is 3933 - identical noise per photosite, which is
exactly why the smaller format is noisier per picture - and the Size property scales the
pitch. The count is capped because no body delivers more than about 8K and every one of
them bins above that; without the cap a "phone" on a full-frame body would be 36 000 sites
across and its noise would integrate away to nothing.

Grain_User_Digital2 joins the bus (raw WB gain red, gain blue, frames stacked, smear),
registered, defaulted, saved and restored like the rest of the block.

r_FilmGrainDebug 3 grows four digital lines: photosite pitch and count, how many photosites
fall under one output pixel and by how much that divides the noise, how many electrons a
mid grey pixel collects and what shot noise alone is worth, the read and fixed-pattern
figures in display-linear units with the white-balance gains, and the resulting stops of
sigma and 8-bit code values on the green channel - so every number in the model can be
checked against what is on screen.

Docs: the README's Film Grain table and a new "digital sensor model" section with the
preset table and the three steps between photosite and pixel; ConsoleReference's
r_FilmGrainDebug 2 and 3 rows and the Grain_User_Digital0/1/2 layouts.

